Give the Deck's own installer screens a route onto the ISO, and settle
encryption where it is decided.

    Target: configs/airootfs/root/configurator   (3 hunks, one file)
    Pairs with: overlay/configs/airootfs/usr/share/omarchy-iso/deck-form.sh
    Spec: docs/tasks/T4-screen-spec.md §1.2 (patch P1);
          docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §1 (the budget) and §5.5 (encryption);
          docs/findings/T4-controller-only-install-first-run.md §13.4/§13.6
          item 1 (the $1 crash, hunk 3, added 2026-08-13)

=== WHY THIS IS THE FIFTH PATCH, AND THE ARGUMENT IT HAS TO MAKE ============

docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §1 point 3 sets the budget at "<= 4 patch files,
and a fifth should have to argue for itself". test/unit/test-iso-build.sh
enforces it mechanically. This is the fifth. The budget was raised to 5 --
deliberately, not to "unbounded" -- and here is the argument, stated plainly
enough to be overruled:

  1. The budget's PURPOSE (§1 point 3, in its own words) is that "patches are
     only allowed for build-time behaviour; everything install-time goes in
     the package", so Phase 4's portable enablement layer (T7) stays
     extractable. This patch does not violate that rule -- it is the one
     class of change the rule cannot cover. deck-form.sh is not install-time
     configuration of a target system; it is the LIVE ISO's own wizard. There
     is no omarchy-deck package on the live ISO to put it in. The package
     ships to the installed Deck, and this code has finished running before
     that package exists.

  2. There is no additive alternative. `configurator` sources exactly one
     external file (setup-form.sh, at a path it hard-codes) and never
     consults a drop-in directory, a hook path, or an environment variable
     for anything else. Overlaying a whole modified `configurator` as an
     additive file was the alternative, and it is strictly worse: a 1196-line
     verbatim copy that upstream edits several times a day, diverging
     silently, which is the exact failure mode §1's "the conflict is the
     point" paragraph exists to avoid. A 2-hunk patch fails loudly on an
     upstream move; a vendored copy does not fail at all.

  3. §1's own patch table already counted this file: "| P1 |
     configs/airootfs/root/configurator | 2 |". P1 was always in the plan.
     What actually pushed the count past four was T4a's dashboard patch,
     added later; the arithmetic changed, the design did not.

  4. It carries three changes, not one. All three are edits to this same
     file, and folding each new one into this patch keeps the budget at 5
     instead of climbing every time this one file needs another line fixed.
     Hunk 3 (added 2026-08-13) is the clearest case yet for that policy: it
     is a single-character fix to a pre-existing upstream bug, discovered
     only because this project's own harness was the first ever to press
     "Install" on a real, survivable disk
     (`docs/findings/T4-controller-only-install-first-run.md` §13.4). A
     sixth patch file for one `${1:-}` guard would be its own small
     absurdity.

  If the operator disagrees: the fallback is not a smaller patch, it is
  shipping an installer whose Wi-Fi passphrase screen, disk screen and
  summary screen do not exist. deck-form.sh is 107KB with 147 passing
  assertions and, before this patch, had shipped on zero ISOs.

=== HUNK 1 -- source deck-form.sh ==========================================

One `source`, immediately before `wait_for_stable_terminal`: after every
function this file and the vendored setup-form.sh define, before any of the
flow runs. Bash keeps the last definition of a name, so deck-form.sh replaces
the screens a controller cannot drive and leaves the rest untouched.

Behind a readability guard that exits 1. `configurator` does not `set -e`, so
an unguarded `source` of a missing file prints one line and CONTINUES into
upstream's keyboard-requiring screens -- the silent failure CLAUDE.md forbids,
on the one screen a Deck owner cannot recover from.

=== HUNK 2 -- deck_final_summary, and encryption OFF =======================

`deck_final_summary || abort` is the second half of spec P1: S5 is a new
screen, not an override, so it must be CALLED. Immediately before
write_user_files is the only place every branch has converged and nothing
below is still a question.

And T5-fork-plan.md §5.5, encryption off by default -- but NOT where §5.5
says to put it. §5.5 names `local mode="encrypted"` at lines 601 and 895
(both line numbers re-verified against iso/upstream this session; both are
right). Both are dead code once hunk 1 lands: deck-form.sh replaces
`confirm_disk_overwrite` outright (line 895's whole function), and its
`requires_full_disk_install() { return 0; }` makes `select_installation` set
full_disk_only=true and skip `install_mode_form`, so "Free space install" can
never be selected and `run_partition_decide` (line 601) is never called.
Flipping those two strings would change no behaviour, and §5.5's own [B]
check -- "grep the patched configurator for mode=unencrypted at both sites"
-- would then pass for the wrong reason, which is this project's named
recurring failure.

So the invariant is enforced at the point of effect instead: one assignment
and one check on the line before write_user_files, downstream of every screen
path and every "go back" loop, immediately above the `if [[
$encrypt_installation == true ]]` that writes the disk_encryption block into
user_configuration.json. It executes on every install. It does not silently
correct a `true` -- it aborts and says why, because encryption here yields a
Deck whose owner has no keyboard to unlock it, and because turning it off
also deletes archinstall's autologin drop-in (§5.1's coupling box), which
configure_deck must restore separately.

=== HUNK 3 -- unguarded $1 at the very end of the file, under set -u =======

Not a Deck-specific screen change -- a one-character fix to a pre-existing
upstream bug, found by
`docs/findings/T4-controller-only-install-first-run.md` §13.4: the FIRST
time any harness in this project (or, as far as that finding's own §13.5
records, apparently anyone) pressed "Install" on a real, correctly-sized,
survivable disk and let `configurator` run all the way to its own last four
lines.

`.automated_script.sh` invokes `./configurator` with zero positional
arguments on every real interactive (non-`cidata`) install
(`iso/upstream/configs/airootfs/root/.automated_script.sh:101`, READ). Two
places in this file guard the resulting unset `$1` correctly --
`[[ "${1:-}" == "dry" ]]` at line 670, `[[ ${1:-} == "dry" ]]` at line 1030 --
but the third, structurally identical check at the true end of the file
(originally line 1194, the only one on the `full_disk` branch a Deck install
can ever take, since `deck-form.sh`'s `requires_full_disk_install` -- hunk 1
-- returns 0 unconditionally) does not: bare `$1`. `configurator` itself has
no `set -u` at its own top, so this was always latent upstream; hunk 1's own
`source /usr/share/omarchy-iso/deck-form.sh` is what turns it fatal, because
`deck-form.sh` sets `set -uo pipefail` at its own top and, being `source`d
into `configurator`'s own shell rather than run in a subshell, that `set -u`
stays live for the rest of `configurator`'s execution, including this line,
much later in the file. So the crash is downstream of hunk 1 (confirming
this belongs in the same patch, not a new file), but the bug being fixed is
plain upstream's, not this fork's -- matched to the two already-correct
sibling checks in the same file, nothing invented.

Measured effect before this fix, `docs/findings/T4-controller-only-install-
first-run.md` §13.4: `write_user_files` runs and its artefacts land on disk,
then `configurator` dies non-zero on this line under `.automated_script.sh`'s
own `set -e` -- before `/usr/local/bin/omarchy-install-dashboard` (the real
installer) is ever invoked, on every controller-only, non-`cidata` full-disk
install, Deck-forked or not.

